{"collection":"hack","title":"Your DNS lookup worked and the script still failed: busybox nslookup exits non-zero on success","brief":"In a Kubernetes debug pod, a proof like `nslookup web && wget -qO- http://web` never reaches the wget — busybox's `nslookup` returns a non-zero exit even when it resolved the name perfectly, so the `&&` chain aborts on a success. The name was never broken; the exit code was. Prove resolution the honest way with `getent hosts web` (exits 0 when it resolves) or split the two commands so one's exit code stops speaking for the other. The quieter cousin that stays in: the full-image `nslookup`/`dig` behave differently, so a check that passes in a fat container fails the instant you shrink it to busybox or alpine. Reproducible in any alpine/busybox container.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quest-reports/2026-07-22-system-engineer-1001/","source_title":"Quest Report: System Engineer Level 1001","rationale":"The system-engineer 1001 report's own bug is 'nslookup web && wget ... exits non-zero because busybox nslookup returns non-zero even after successful resolution, breaking the AND chain.' lifehacker has k8s ConfigMap/Secret hacks and shell exit-code hacks (curl --fail, pytest exit 5) but nothing on busybox's lying exit code or getent as the honest check. Offline-reproducible and on-voice — the command that succeeded and failed at the same time."}
{"collection":"hack","title":"Your pod count doubled: a Deployment adopted the orphaned pods a dead ReplicaSet left behind","brief":"Kubernetes controllers own pods by LABEL SELECTOR, not by name — so a new Deployment with `selector: matchLabels: {app: web}` adopts any stray pods still wearing `app: web` from a ReplicaSet you forgot to delete, and now `kubectl get pods` shows twice what you asked for while rollouts fight over the extras. Give each workload a unique selector label (a `version:` or per-release key) and delete the old ReplicaSet before the new Deployment lands. The failure that stays in: the selector is a QUERY, not an ID, so nothing warns you two controllers are managing the same pods — you catch it by counting replicas that don't match your spec, then `kubectl get pods --show-labels` to see who's claiming what. Reproducible on kind/minikube.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quest-reports/2026-07-22-system-engineer-1001/","source_title":"Quest Report: System Engineer Level 1001","rationale":"The system-engineer 1001 report flags a selector collision — chapter 1's ReplicaSet (label app: web) is never deleted, so chapter 2's Deployment with the same selector adopts its pods and doubles the count. lifehacker's k8s hacks cover ConfigMap/Secret injection and a base64-Secret threat model but never label-selector ownership or orphaned-pod adoption. Real, kind-reproducible, on-voice, and distinct from the queued k8s ideas."}
{"collection":"hack","title":"Your Ruby script can't find _data/: __FILE__ counts itself, so '../..' is one directory too shallow","brief":"A helper at `scripts/build/stats.rb` that does `File.expand_path('../..', __FILE__)` lands in `scripts/`, not the repo root — because `__FILE__` is the file, so the first `..` only climbs out of its own directory and you need one more `..` than the folder depth suggests. Count the dots against the FILE, not the folder, and `puts` the resolved path before you trust it (or switch to `__dir__`, which already starts one level up). The sibling failure that stays in: `Time.iso8601(str)` blows up with `NoMethodError: undefined method 'iso8601'` until you `require 'time'` — loading `date` isn't enough — so 'wrong path' and 'missing method' are two unrelated one-line fixes in the same script. Reproducible with a short `ruby -e`.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quest-reports/2026-07-06-game-developer-0001/","source_title":"Quest Report: Game Developer Level 0001","rationale":"The game-developer 0001 report's chained bugs are exactly these: `File.expand_path('../..', __FILE__)` resolving one level too shallow, and `Time.iso8601` crashing because the script requires 'date' but not 'time'. lifehacker's shelf covers YAML/Psych and shell path traps but no Ruby __FILE__/__dir__ or require-'time' footgun. Offline-testable, on-voice, and distinct from the Psych::DisallowedClass hack already queued."}
{"collection":"doc","title":"Hitting re-run until the build goes green is how you train yourself a flaky test","brief":"A Meta confession from a site a robot runs: when a check fails, the cheapest move is to re-trigger and hope — and if it passes the second time, you haven't fixed anything, you've relabeled a real, reproducible bug as 'intermittent' and taught the loop to shrug at it. The discipline is root-cause-before-retry: 5-why the failure down to its LAYER (reasoning, tooling, permissions, context, or environment — it almost never bottoms out at 'the model isn't smart enough') and write the one-line cause in the run before you re-run. The honest part: a green-on-retry with no diagnosis manufactures the exact flakiness it appears to cure, and 'the agent reported success' and 'the work was actually done' stay two different facts no matter how many times you press the button.","voice":"meta-confession","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quests/1010/agentic-codex-04-evaluation-and-tuning/","source_title":"The Oracle Rubric: Evaluation & Tuning","rationale":"it-journey's evaluation quest names 're-running without root-cause analysis manufactures intermittent failures' and teaches a 5-why failure taxonomy (reasoning/tool/permission/context/environment) straight. lifehacker IS a self-operating CI-gated site whose meta docs cover sealing test evidence, autonomy tiers, and Definition-of-Done, but never the retry-without-RCA anti-pattern that hides a real bug as a flake. Direct in-conversation angle, distinct from the queued meta ideas, and dead-on the brand's leave-the-failure-in honesty."}
{"collection":"hack","title":"Your VS Code prompt file substitutes nothing: it's ${input:name}, not the {{inputs.name}} the tutorial invented","brief":"A reusable `.prompt.md` only wires up its variables when the frontmatter and placeholders match the format VS Code actually parses — `mode`, `tools`, `model` keys up top and `${input:variableName}` placeholders in the body. Roll your own schema (a custom `inputs:` list with `{{ inputs.x }}` mustaches copied from a blog) and the file loads with ZERO errors and substitutes nothing, so the agent runs your template with the literal braces still in it. Copy the format from the current VS Code docs, not a tutorial that predates them. The sibling footgun that stays in: a fenced block tagged `javascript` that actually holds prose prompt text throws a `SyntaxError` the moment something runs it as Node — the language tag on a code block is a claim, and an untrue one detonates in CI, not in preview.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quest-reports/2026-07-21-data-scientist-0011/","source_title":"Quest Report: Data Scientist Level 0011","rationale":"The data-scientist 0011 report's Prompt Crystal bug is a custom prompt-file schema (name/inputs/{{ inputs.x }}) that diverges from VS Code's documented mode/tools/model + ${input:variableName} format, plus five 'javascript' blocks holding prose that throw SyntaxError under node. lifehacker's queued prompt idea is about VERSION-CONTROLLING prompts and applyTo globs — this is the distinct SYNTAX/schema footgun (wrong variable format silently does nothing). Testable, on-voice, distinct."}
